---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro'
import Capture from '../../components/Capture.astro'
import { doc, shot } from '../../lib/content'
const sections: [string, string][] = [
  ['setup', '1. Connect your machine'],
  ['control', '2. Open a remote lane'],
  ['browser', '3. Use the browser or phone'],
  ['moves', 'Move a lane deliberately'],
  ['limits', 'Recovery and boundaries'],
]
---

<Guide
  title="Your machines. One working view."
  description="Run pi on a computer you own and reach it from Desktop or the browser. The Host owns execution; Phosphor’s control service handles identity, not your code."
  sections={sections}
  eyebrow="Guide / Machines and PWA"
>
  <section id="setup">
    <h2>1. Bring a machine online</h2>
    <p>
      This is private remote access, not a cloud runner. Local Desktop lanes need no Phosphor
      account. Remote access needs GitHub sign-in to Phosphor, an execution machine with pi, and
      Tailscale on both computers on the same tailnet. Enable MagicDNS and HTTPS certificates.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        In Desktop, sign in and open <strong>Machines → Add a machine</strong>. Follow the readiness
        checks and copy its signed Host install command to the execution machine.
      </li>
      <li>
        The command runs <code>phosphor setup</code>, prepares the service and Tailscale Serve, and
        prints an enrollment code and fingerprint.
      </li>
      <li>
        Choose <strong>I have a code</strong> in Desktop, enter the code, compare the fingerprint and
        approve the machine.
      </li>
      <li>
        Open that machine’s Provider settings, or run <code>phosphor providers login PROVIDER</code> on
        the Host. Add a repository there. Credentials stay on the execution machine.
      </li>
      <li>
        Check readiness; <code>phosphor doctor</code> diagnoses setup. An online directory row alone is
        not permission to execute or proof that pi is ready.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <Capture
      image={shot('machines')}
      alt="Machines showing fictional computers and their connection readiness in Phosphor."
      caption="Know where the next lane will run"
    />
    <p>
      Use Tailscale Serve, never Funnel or a public tunnel. Lane traffic goes directly over private
      HTTPS/WSS to the Host, not through the control service. <a href={doc('host.md#commands')}
        >Host installation and command reference</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="control">
    <h2>2. Open a lane on that Host</h2>
    <p>
      Select the machine and one of its repositories, choose an available model and create a lane.
      Its files, provider accounts, tools and processes belong to that Host. If the Host is
      unavailable, Phosphor does not quietly start a local lane instead.
    </p>
    <p>
      A lane has one controller. Reading it does not acquire control. Use <strong
        >Take control</strong
      > before prompting or changing it, and release control explicitly when handing it over. Disconnecting
      does not release control.
    </p>
    <p>
      Chat, files, changes, terminals, artifacts and routines use the Host’s supported capabilities.
      Machine configuration requires a separate <strong>Enable configuration access</strong> confirmation.
      This is not automatic synchronization of local settings or credentials.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="browser">
    <h2>3. Check in from the browser or phone</h2>
    <p>
      Open the <a href="https://phosphor-app.saccolabs.com/">Phosphor web app</a> on a device that can
      reach your private tailnet and sign in. The Host must allow the app’s browser origin. You can install
      the PWA from Settings → App installation where your browser supports it.
    </p>
    <Capture
      image={shot('mobile')}
      alt="Phosphor’s phone layout showing a fictional Host lane with shared chat controls."
      caption="A smaller screen, the same Host lane"
      sizes="(max-width: 700px) 90vw, 420px"
      class="phone-capture"
    />
    <p>
      On a phone, Home shows lanes that need you, Machines selects computers, and Library holds
      Skills, Routines and Artifacts. Inside a lane, its title opens the pane and session menu; PR
      and Slack pills remain directly accessible.
    </p>
    <p>
      The browser is remote-only. It has no This Mac lanes, native file editor, split panes or
      Brain. It is not an offline execution environment. Configuration access lasts only for the
      current browser page; closing or reloading it clears that opt-in.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="moves">
    <h2>Move a lane, not a running process</h2>
    <p>
      Desktop’s lane menu offers <strong>Move to…</strong> when work is idle. It stops an idle open lane
      first, then shows a checked plan for a destination machine and workspace. The browser cannot relay
      these transfers.
    </p>
    <p>
      A move is a snapshot copy: the original stays untouched and the destination is not resumed
      automatically. It preserves the saved conversation, artifact versions and child-run history.
      Choose Open to continue at the destination, and read the notice about old paths and moved
      attachments.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Commit tracked and untracked changes first; dirty Git work blocks a transfer. Ignored files
        do not move.
      </li>
      <li>
        For the same origin on another machine, the destination needs the source branch’s exact
        commit. For a different Git workspace, a fresh branch starts from that destination’s default
        branch; source code stays behind.
      </li>
      <li>
        Provider/model availability, pi version and destination setup are checked. Skills,
        extensions, MCP and accounts belong to the destination.
      </li>
      <li>
        Workspace files, credentials, terminal processes and routine schedules are not copied. For
        the same repository on the same machine, use Clone instead.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Keep both Hosts connected for a Host-to-Host move. If the outcome is unconfirmed, inspect the
      destination before retrying; it is not permission to copy again.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="limits">
    <h2>Keep the execution boundary visible</h2>
    <p>
      The control service stores identity and machine metadata, never lane transcripts, repository
      paths or provider credentials. Tools still have the execution machine’s access: private
      transport and controller grants are not an agent sandbox.
    </p>
    <p>
      Connection loss disables actions; reconnect and inspect state before repeating an uncertain
      operation. Host routines run on the Host and need it awake with its service running. Brain
      remains local to Desktop and does not follow a lane transfer.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={doc('remote-access.md')}>Remote access contract</a> · <a href={doc('web-client.md')}
        >Browser capabilities</a
      > · <a href={doc('lanes.md#moving-a-lane')}>Transfer checks and limits</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
